Defer parent-mismatch detection

drain_equalities pops pending equalities one at a time. If a member-el
equate (e.g. t_a = t_b, parents [e_a] vs [e_b]) is processed before
the equate that would unify the parents (e_a = e_b), the parent
disagreement looked like a real conflict and got reported. Reordering
the queue would have made the same input pass cleanly. The same race
existed in impose_concrete_type, since typing runs interleaved with
drain_equalities inside the close fixed point.

Add a pending_parent_checks queue to Structure holding
DeferredParentCheck entries. drain_equalities and impose_concrete_type
now push onto this queue when only the parents disagree (typ
mismatches stay immediate, since TypeId is immutable). After the
drain/functionality/typing fixed point converges, Structure::close
walks the deferred checks under the final unification and only emits
a TypeConflict for entries whose parents still disagree.

Deferred checks do not feed back into the fixed point — they are
terminal observations. The queue is mem::taken at the end so each
Structure::close call (e.g. forward and backward pass in
StructureCat::close) starts fresh.
